@page "/field-state"

<PageTitle>Field-state visualizer</PageTitle>
<h1>Field-state visualizer</h1>
<p>Every CSS class and pending indicator the kit renders is a decision made from one small record: <code>FieldState</code>. This page puts it on screen directly, live, for both fields below.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor", "FieldStateVisualizer.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Touched</strong>: the user has interacted with the field, or a draft load decided for it.</li>
            <li><strong>Modified</strong>: the EditContext says the value changed.</li>
            <li><strong>Validating</strong>: a validation pass involving this field is in flight; scoped to the field for live/refresh passes and form-wide for a submit, the pass you asked for. A draft load stays false for every field: it answers for the whole model, so the engine's own flag is true for a page-level spinner, but no field is waiting on it.</li>
            <li><strong>Errors</strong>: the field currently carries error-severity messages.</li>
            <li><strong>Warnings</strong>: the field currently carries warning-severity messages.</li>
            <li><strong>Blur</strong> has no column of its own, and no flag either: it is what marks touched on this page, wiring <code>field.MarkTouched()</code> to <code>onblur</code> — the context seam meant for custom inputs. A built-in input marks touched only when a committed value change notifies the engine, in every <code>UpdateOn</code> mode, so without this wiring, tabbing through never touches.</li>
            <li><strong>Form valid</strong>: the readout above Submit, kept live by <code>FormidableOptions.TrackFormValidity</code> — an invisible Submit-profile probe that runs once at construction and again on every change, executes only the rules still owed an answer in the engine's verdict store, and feeds what it ran back into that store for the passes beside it to reuse. Submit stays disabled until the whole form would pass.</li>
            <li>What the probe earns on this page is narrower than the option sounds, because the live pass beside it already evaluates the submit profile's rules and lands the same answers in the same store. What it adds here is the answer <em>before</em> any edit: a pristine form that reports truthfully, and the "confirmed" border on a field you have only tabbed through while nothing on the form has been changed yet. Two other shapes need it for much more — a form that narrows <code>LiveProfile</code>, where no live pass ever answers the submit rules, and a validator that cannot execute rule by rule, where a live pass is never taken as a coverage source at all. Green means the engine can vouch a submit would not fail the field, not merely that you visited it, whichever pass put the answer in the store.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Tab into Username and out — Touched flips to True, Modified stays False. Nothing was typed, so nothing was committed: the blur alone touches the field, because this page wires <code>field.MarkTouched()</code> to <code>onblur</code>.</li>
            <li>Look at Username's border now — it takes none, and Submit stays disabled with Form valid reading No. The probe's answer already carries the required-Username failure, and a field the engine knows would fail submit is never styled valid, disclosed message or none.</li>
            <li>Tab through Display name the same way and it DOES confirm: the same answer holds nothing against it. Nothing has been edited yet, so no live pass has run for either field — this is the probe's work alone.</li>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> — Modified and Validating flip to True, then Errors flips once the taken-check lands. Submit stays disabled: a taken username still fails the probe.</li>
            <li>Clear Username — the field is engaged now, so "Username is required" appears inline at once, from the live pass rather than from any submit.</li>
            <li>Type <code>ada</code> instead: once the async check clears, the message goes, Form valid flips to Yes and Submit enables itself with no click needed. Tab out and the border confirms too — a focused box wears the accent border whatever its verdict, so that one waits for the blur. What it confirms is that the store holds a fresh, clean submit answer for the field, whichever of the probe and the live pass got there first.</li>
            <li>Submit — both rows' Validating flip together: submit is a form-wide pass, not a per-field one.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_handle" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Username <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
    </FormidableField>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" @onblur="() => field.MarkTouched()" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
    </FormidableField>

    <table class="state-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>Touched</th>
                <th>Modified</th>
                <th>Validating</th>
                <th>Errors</th>
                <th>Warnings</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
                <tr>
                    <td>Username</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                </tr>
            </FormidableField>
            <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
                <tr>
                    <td>Display name</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsTouched</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsModified</td>
                    <td>@field.State.IsValidating</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasErrors</td>
                    <td>@field.State.HasWarnings</td>
                </tr>
            </FormidableField>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Form valid: <strong>@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid == true ? "Yes" : "No")</strong></p>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit" disabled="@(_form?.Engine?.IsFormValid != true)">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<p><small>The table rows above are not a read-only view bolted on the side — each is its own <code>FormidableField</code>, registered with the form exactly like the inputs above it. That is why they re-render the instant a flag changes: render-registration means any markup a field renders, table row included, is the live proof of that field's state.</small></p>
